\section{Master bounding box}{

Points are only drawn from the part of the polygon that lies inside
\code{master_bbox}.
If the polygon extends beyond the box, that part is never
sampled and a warning is issued.
The \code{n} requested points are still returned, spread over the area inside the
box.
They are not an equal-probability sample of the whole polygon.

If the polygon only touches the box without overlapping it, the call fails
(\code{"routing"}) or can scan a very long stretch of the sequence before
failing (\code{"acceptance sampling"}).

With the same \code{master_bbox}, \code{seed} and \code{algorithm}, the sequence domain is
identical for different polygons.
Spatial consistency across polygons holds for \code{"acceptance sampling"}, but
not for \code{"routing"} or \code{"warp"}, which depends on the exact area of each
polygon.
}

\section{Area-equal sampling}{

Longitude is mapped linearly from the Sobol stream.
Authalic latitude is mapped through the inverse CDF of \code{sin(lat)} restricted
to the bounding box latitude band, so that the expected number of candidate
points per unit surface area is uniform across the globe regardless of
latitude.
}

\section{Coordinate system}{

Input and output are in longitude, latitude decimal degrees without assuming
a specific datum.
Projection to planar coordinates is not required.
However, polygons straddling the antimeridian (180°/-180°) must be
pre-split into a \code{MULTIPOLYGON} (e.g., using \code{sf::st_wrap_dateline()}) before
conversion to WKB.
}

\section{Prefix stability}{

Calling with the same \code{seed} and a larger sample size \code{n2 > n1} on the
\strong{same polygon} produces a sample whose first \code{n1} rows are identical to the
\code{n1} row result.
}

